PLATFORM NOTES
Friday, March 13, 2026

Regulation as a wedge

Founders treat regulation as weather: something that happens to the industry, to be endured. The better founders treat it as terrain. Every new rule creates a deadline, and every deadline creates a buyer who did not exist the day before and who has a budget line and a date.

The pattern repeats across industries. A traceability requirement in food creates a market for lot-tracking software. A reporting obligation in finance creates a market for the reporting. An emissions rule creates a market for the measuring. In each case the incumbents, who have the customers, respond slowly, because the rule is one of forty things on their roadmap. The startup, for which the rule is the whole roadmap, ships first and sells against the deadline.

Two cautions from watching this play out. First, the deadline moves. Regulators extend, courts delay, and a company that built its pipeline around a date can find the date gone. The defense is to sell the product on its operational merit and treat the deadline as the reason to buy this quarter rather than the reason to buy at all. Second, the market created by a rule is finite. Once everyone is compliant, the compliance product is a maintenance business unless it has become something else along the way.

The companies that get this right use the regulatory wedge to get in the door and the operational product to stay. The compliance module is the first purchase; the workflow it is bolted to is the second, and the second is the business.

